<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search (ANNS) plays a critical role in applications such as search engines, recommender systems, and RAG for LLMs. Vector quantization (VQ), a crucial technique for ANNS, is commonly used to reduce space overhead and accelerate distance computations. However, despite significant research advances, state-of-the-art VQ methods still face challenges in balancing encoding efficiency and quantization accuracy. To address these limitations, we propose a novel VQ method called SAQ. To improve accuracy, SAQ employs a new dimension segmentation technique to strategically partition PCA-projected vectors into segments along their dimensions. By prioritizing leading dimension segments with larger magnitudes, SAQ allocates more bits to high-impact segments, optimizing the use of the available space quota. An efficient dynamic programming algorithm is developed to optimize dimension segmentation and bit allocation, ensuring minimal quantization error. To speed up vector encoding, SAQ devises a code adjustment technique to first quantize each dimension independently and then progressively refine quantized vectors using a coordinate-descent-like approach to avoid exhaustive enumeration. Extensive experiments demonstrate SAQ’s superiority over classical methods (e.g., PQ, PCA) and recent state-of-the-art approaches (e.g., LVQ, Extended RabitQ). SAQ achieves up to 80% reduction in quantization error and accelerates encoding speed by over 80× compared to Extended RabitQ.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Vector quantization, nearest neighbor search, vector database
</div><span id="id11" class="ltx_note ltx_role_cc-license"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">cc-license: </span>by-sa</span></span></span>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Artifact Availability:
<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The source code, data, and/or other artifacts have been made available at </span><a href="https://github.com/howarlii/saq/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">https://github.com/howarlii/saq/</a><span id="p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the proliferation of machine learning, embedding models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shvetsova et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> are widely used to map diverse data objects, including images, videos, texts, e-commerce goods, genes, and proteins, to high-dimensional vector embeddings that encode their semantic information. A core operation on these vectors is <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nearest neighbor search</span> (<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NNS</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elastic, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">pgvector, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">SingleStore, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qdrant, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohoney et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which retrieves vectors that are the most similar to a query vector from a vector dataset. NNS underpins many important applications including content search (e.g., for images and videos), recommender systems, bio-informatics, and retrieval-argumented generation (RAG) for LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
However, exact NNS requires a linear scan in high-dimensional space, rendering it impractical for large-scale datasets. To address this, <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">approximate NNS</span> (<span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ANNS</span>) has been widely adopted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which trades exactness for efficiency by returning most of the top-<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nearest neighbors. Many vector indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malkov and Yashunin, 2018b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and vector databases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elastic, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">pgvector, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">SingleStore, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qdrant, </a>)</cite> support ANNS as the core functionality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Vector quantization (or vector compression) maps each vector <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> to a smaller approximate vector <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> and is a key technique for efficient ANNS. With billions of vectors and each vector having thousands of dimensions, vector datasets can be large (e.g., in TBs) and vector quantization helps reduce space consumption. Moreover, vector quantization enables us to compute approximate distance (i.e., <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{q}-\bar{\mathbf{o}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{q}-\bar{\mathbf{o}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the query vector) faster than the exact distance (i.e., <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|</annotation></semantics></math>). Since distance computation dominates the running time of vector indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Long, 2023</a>)</cite>, vector quantization effectively accelerates ANNS.
The goal of vector quantization is to minimize the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative error</span> (defined as <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\lvert\|\mathbf{q}-\bar{\mathbf{o}}\|^{2}-\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|^{2}\big\rvert/\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\lvert\|\mathbf{q}-\bar{\mathbf{o}}\|^{2}-\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|^{2}\big\rvert/\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{o}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) under a given space quota (i.e., measured by compression ratio w.r.t. the original vector or the average number of bits used for each dimension) such that approximate distance matches exact distance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12086v2/alpha-fig.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/248;" width="405" height="248" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Illustration of dimension balancing and dimension reduction. Bar height is the magnitude of vector dimension.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Many vector quantization methods have been proposed.
PQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babenko and Lempitsky, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jegou et al., 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martinez et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, OPQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, and LOPQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalantidis and Avrithis, 2014</a>)</cite> partition the <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space into subspaces and use K-means to learn vector codebooks for each subspace. AQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babenko and Lempitsky, 2014</a>)</cite>, RQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2010</a>)</cite>, LSQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martinez et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martinez et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and TreeQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foote, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Charami et al., 2007</a>)</cite> further improve the structures and learning methods of the vector codebooks. Some other methods learn query-dependent codebooks to better approximate the query-vector distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. The state-of-the-art vector quantization methods adopt two types of designs as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The first one is <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension balancing</span>, which uses a random orthonormal matrix <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to project a vector <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and then quantizes each dimension of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rx</annotation></semantics></math> to an integer. The representative is RaBitQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Long, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which quantizes each projected vector dimension with 1 bit and provides unbiased distance estimation. The second type is <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension reduction</span>, which uses a PCA projection matrix <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to project a vector <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> such that the leading dimensions correspond to large eigenvalues and thus have large magnitudes. Then, the small tailing dimensions are discarded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the progresses, we observe two limitations in the state-of-the-art vector quantization methods.</p>
<ul id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High quantization complexity</span>: Currently, RaBitQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Long, 2024</a>)</cite> and extended RaBitQ (E-RaBitQ) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieve the best accuracy. However, RaBitQ can only use 1 bit for each vector dimension, which limits accuracy, while the quantization complexity of E-RaBitQ is <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})</annotation></semantics></math> where a vector has <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions and each dimension uses <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> bits. This is because E-RaBitQ does not handle each vector dimension independently, but an enumeration is required to decide the optimal quantized vector to approximate the original vector. Our profiling shows that when <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3,072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3,072</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3,072</annotation></semantics></math>, quantizing 1 billion vectors with E-RaBitQ takes more than 3,600 CPU hours.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contradictory designs</span>: Dimension balancing attempts to make vector dimensions similar in magnitude such that the same number of bits can be used for each dimension. In contrast, dimension reduction makes the vector dimensions skewed in magnitude so that a vector can be approximated by its leading dimensions. These two designs are fundamentally contradictory, posing problems about selecting the better method for different applications or considering synergistic potential for enhanced performance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The inherent conflict between the two dominant design paradigms in existing quantization methods makes it difficult to further improve the efficiency of quantization while retaining the accuracy of vector search. To resolve this challenge, we propose a novel vector quantization method, <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SAQ</span>, that overcomes the limitations of state-of-the-art methods through two key innovations: <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">code adjustment</span> and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension segmentation</span>. One striking contribution of SAQ is: SAQ significantly advances <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both</span> quantization efficiency and vector search accuracy of currently best methods. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, SAQ achieves significantly lower quantization errors than RaBitQ<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          In the subsequent discussion, we also refer to E-RaBitQ as RabitQ for simplicity as most of our discussions are related to E-RaBitQ.</span></span></span> with the same space quota, while delivering dramatic speed-ups of over 80x faster quantization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">SAQ introduces a <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">code adjustment</span> technique to accelerate vector quantization. In particular, after random orthonormal projection, SAQ first quantizes each dimension of a vector independently using <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> bits. Observing that quantization accuracy depends on how well the original vector <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and quantized vector <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> align in their directions (measured by cosine similarity), we propose to adjust the quantized code for each dimension of <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to better align with <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields a complexity of <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(D)</annotation></semantics></math> to quantize each vector, avoiding RaBitQ’s code enumeration which requires <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call the new quantization method <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CAQ</span> and show that CAQ achieves the same quantization accuracy as RaBitQ, as code adjustment essentially conducts coordinate descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wright, 2015</a>)</cite> to optimize <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">SAQ utilizes a <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension segmentation</span> technique to bridge dimension reduction and dimension balancing for improved accuracy.
After PCA projection, the vector dimensions are partitioned into segments, and CAQ is applied independently to each dimension segment. The key is to allocate more bits to the segments of leading (high-magnitude) dimensions to improve accuracy, while segments of trailing dimensions use fewer bits or are discarded.
We develop a dynamic programming algorithm to optimally allocate bits across dimension segments to minimize quantization error under a total space quota <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cdot D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cdot D</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that dimension segmentation ensures that SAQ’s approximation error cannot be larger than RaBitQ’s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We further enhance SAQ with a set of novel optimizations. First, we design a distance estimators based on the quantized vectors and develop single-instruction-multiple-data (SIMD) implementations for efficient distance computation. Second, CAQ supports <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">progressive distance approximation</span>, enabling us to take the first <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;B</annotation></semantics></math> bits for each dimension while still yielding a valid quantized vector (with reduced accuracy). Third, SAQ supports multi-stage distance estimation, which gives lower and upper bounds for distances using the segmented approximate vectors. The two properties are useful for pruning in ANNS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Long, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments to evaluate SAQ and compare it with four representative vector quantization methods. The results show that under the same space quota, SAQ consistently achieves lower approximation error than the baselines. In particular, to achieve the same accuracy as 8-bit RaBitQ, SAQ only needs 5-6 bits for each dimension, which is also shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. When both RaBitQ and SAQ use 8 bits for each dimension, the approximation errors of SAQ are usually below 50% of E-RaBitQ. Moreover, SAQ can accelerate the vector quantization process of RaBitQ by up to 80x. We also show that SAQ’s accuracy gains translate to higher query throughput for ANNS, and that our two key designs, i.e., code adjustment and dimension segmentation, are effective.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12086v2/error_vs_B_bit_matrix_np200_tiny.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/217;" width="286" height="217"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The vector approximation error of SAQ and representative baselines for the GIST dataset. Note that RaBitQ refers to extended RaBitQ (same for other experiments). </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify the critical limitations in state-of-the-art vector quantization methods, i.e., prolonged quantization time and contradictory design paradigms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose SAQ, which pushes the limits of current vector quantization methods in both accuracy and efficiency. SAQ comes with two key designs: code adjustment for linear-time quantization and dimension segmentation for optimal bit allocation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a set of novel optimizations for SAQ that includes the multi-stage distance estimator, distance bounds, dynamic programming for bit allocation, and SIMD implementations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I2.i4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive experimental evaluation to demonstrate SAQ’s superiority in accuracy (up to 5.4× improvement), quantization speed (up to 80× faster), and ANNS performance (up to 12.5x higher query throughput at 95% recall).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we introduce the basics of RaBitQ and extended RaBitQ to facilitate the subsequent discussions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Locally-adaptive Vector Quantization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Locally-adaptive Vector Quantization (LVQ) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguerrebere et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a recently proposed vector quantization technique that efficiently compresses high-dimensional vectors while preserving the accuracy of similarity computations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each vector <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=[x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math>, LVQ first mean-centers the vector by subtracting its mean <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> resulting in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\mu}=[\mu_{1},...,\mu_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\mu}=[\mu_{1},...,\mu_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean of all vectors in a dataset. It then computes the minimum and maximum values of the mean-centered vector, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\min_{j}x^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\min_{j}x^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\max_{j}x^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\max_{j}x^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and divides the range <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell,u]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell,u]</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> equal intervals, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of bits per dimension. Each coordinate <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantized to the nearest interval center:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(x^{\prime}_{j};B,\ell,u)=\ell+\Delta\left\lfloor\frac{x^{\prime}_{j}-\ell}{\Delta}+\frac{1}{2}\right\rfloor,\quad\text{where }\Delta=\frac{u-\ell}{2^{B}-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(x^{\prime}_{j};B,\ell,u)=\ell+\Delta\left\lfloor\frac{x^{\prime}_{j}-\ell}{\Delta}+\frac{1}{2}\right\rfloor,\quad\text{where }\Delta=\frac{u-\ell}{2^{B}-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The quantized codes for all dimensions, together with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, are stored for each vector. LVQ is simple and efficient, and by adapting the quantization range to each vector, it can achieve better accuracy than global quantization under the same bit budget. However, its accuracy may degrade under high compression rates due to the limited dynamic range per vector.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>RaBitQ</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RaBitQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Long, 2024</a>)</cite> compresses a <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> into a <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-bit string and two floating point numbers. It provides an unbiased estimator for squared Euclidean distance and supports efficient distance computation with bit operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantization procedure.</span> Given a data vector <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query vector <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, RaBitQ first normalizes them based on a reference vector <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the centroid of the dataset or a cluster). The normalized vectors are expressed as</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}:=\frac{\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|},\quad\mathbf{q}:=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}:=\frac{\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|},\quad\mathbf{q}:=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">RaBitQ estimates the inner product between the normalized vectors (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{o},\mathbf{q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{o},\mathbf{q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>) and computes the Euclidean distance between the original vectors (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) as:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{q}_{r}\|^{2}=\|(\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c})-(\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{q}_{r}\|^{2}=\|(\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c})-(\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|^{2}+\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|^{2}-2\cdot\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|\cdot\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|\cdot\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|^{2}+\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|^{2}-2\cdot\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|\cdot\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|\cdot\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|</annotation></semantics></math> are the distances of the data and query vectors to the reference vector <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be precomputed prior to distance computation and reused. As such, RaBitQ quantizes the normalized data vector <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and focuses on estimating <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To conduct quantization, RaBitQ first generates a random orthonormal matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and projects the normalized data vector <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            RaBitQ actually projects <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> but this is equivalent since <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a random orthonormal matrix.</span></span></span> that is, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}:=P\cdot\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}:=P\cdot\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is orthonormal, it preserves inner product, i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{q}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle=\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{q}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle=\langle\mathbf{q},\mathbf{o}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, we can estimate compress <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and estimate <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{q}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{q}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> has unit norm, RaBitQ uses the following codebook <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:=\left\{+\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}},-\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}}\right\}^{D}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:=\left\{+\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}},-\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}}\right\}^{D},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where each codeword is also a unit vector, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantized to its nearest codeword in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This essentially becomes selecting between -1 and +1 according to the sign for each dimension of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is also natural since after random orthonormal projection, the dimensions of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> follow the same distribution, and the same number of bits should be used to quantize each dimension. The quantized vector is denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distance estimator.</span> To estimate distance, RaBitQ first rotates the normalized query vector with the random orthonormal matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{p}:=P\cdot\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{p}:=P\cdot\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, it estimates <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle=\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle=\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the quantized data vector, <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is computed at query time, and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is computed offline and stored for each vector. It has been shown that the estimator is unbiased and have tight error bound. We restate the lemmas as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 2.1 (Estimator and Error Bound).</h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The estimator of inner product is unbiased because :</span></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left(\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle\right)=\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left(\langle\mathbf{o}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle\right)=\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With a probability of at least <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\exp(-c_{0}\epsilon_{0}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\exp(-c_{0}\epsilon_{0}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the error bound of the estimator satisfies
<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left\{\left|\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle}-\langle\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime},\mathbf{q}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle\right|&gt;\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle^{2}}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle^{2}}}\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{0}}{\sqrt{D-1}}\right\}\leq 2e^{-c_{0}\epsilon_{0}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐪</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐪</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐨</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left\{\left|\frac{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{q}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle}-\langle\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime},\mathbf{q}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle\right|&gt;\sqrt{\frac{1-\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle^{2}}{\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}^{\prime}\rangle^{2}}}\cdot\frac{\epsilon_{0}}{\sqrt{D-1}}\right\}\leq 2e^{-c_{0}\epsilon_{0}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter that controls the probability of failure of the bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is also shown that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p},\mathbf{o}_{p}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is highly concentrated around <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, and the estimation error is smaller than <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{D})</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alon and Klartag, 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Extended RaBitQ</h3>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12086v2/figures/rbq-codebook.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/188;" width="381" height="188" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The codebook structure of extended RaBitQ with dimension <math id="S2.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> bits for each dimension. Red points are the final codewords, Figure reproduced from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RaBitQ only allows to use 1-bit for each vector dimension, which limits accuracy. To improve accuracy, it pads <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> with zeros to a dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}&gt;D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}&gt;D</annotation></semantics></math> and uses <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> bits for quantization. However, this is shown to be sub-optimal, and extended RaBitQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (denoted as E-RaBitQ) is proposed to support <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-bit quantization for each vector dimension, where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer. In particular, E-RaBitQ quantizes a <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> into a <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D*B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D*B)</annotation></semantics></math>-bit string and two floating point numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The normalization and projection of E-RaBitQ are the same as RaBitQ but E-RaBitQ uses the following codebook <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to quantize <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="S2.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{G}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒢</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{G}:=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{-\frac{2^{B}-1}{2}+u\,\middle|\,u=0,1,2,3,...,2^{B}-1\right\}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo>}</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{-\frac{2^{B}-1}{2}+u\,\middle|\,u=0,1,2,3,...,2^{B}-1\right\}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{G}_{r}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒢</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">r</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{G}_{r}:=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\frac{\mathbf{y}}{\left\|\mathbf{y}\right\|}\middle|\mathbf{y}\in G\right\}^{D}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">G</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\frac{\mathbf{y}}{\left\|\mathbf{y}\right\|}\middle|\mathbf{y}\in G\right\}^{D}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.3. Extended RaBitQ ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the raw codewords form a regular grid in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, while the actual codewords are scaled to the unit sphere to have unit norm. This is because <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> also has unit norm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A data vector <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> finds the nearest codeword in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as its quantized vector and stores the corresponding quantization code <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{b}\in\left\{0,1,2,3,...,2^{B}-1\right\}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{b}\in\left\{0,1,2,3,...,2^{B}-1\right\}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> for the codeword. However, it is difficult to find the neatest codeword in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. E-RaBitQ propose a pruned enumeration algorithm to search the codeword with a time complexity of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, the quantization time can be long with using a few bits (e.g. <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>) for each dimension for high accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The distance estimator of E-RaBitQ is the same as RaBitQ, and its error bound is shown empirically as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Remark 1 (Error Bound).</h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> be the absolute error in estimating the inner product of unit vectors. With &gt;99.9% probability, we have <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&lt;2^{-B}\cdot c_{\epsilon}/\sqrt{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&lt;2^{-B}\cdot c_{\epsilon}/\sqrt{D}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\epsilon}=5.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\epsilon}=5.75</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This is asymptotically optimal as the error scales with <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It has been shown that when <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, taking the most significant bit for each dimension can get the quantized vector of the original RaBitQ. As such, E-RaBitQ proposes a progressive strategy to accelerate vector search, which first uses the most significant bits of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute bounds for the distance, and full-precession computation is used only when <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is likely to have smaller distance than the current top-<math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors. However, taking <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;B</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> bits from <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessary form a valid quantized vector. In the subsequent paper, we denote E-RaBitQ as RaBitQ for simplicity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Code Adjustment</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Extended RaBitQ ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, RaBitQ has a complexity of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})</annotation></semantics></math> for encoding a <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimension vector with <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\cdot D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\cdot D</annotation></semantics></math> bits. Therefore, vector quantization can be time consuming. For example, for a dataset with a billion vectors, <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3072</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3072</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=9</annotation></semantics></math>, RaBitQ takes more than 3,600 CPU hours. As we mentioned in Section <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, SAQ segments dimensions and uses more bits for segments with large magnitudes. If we directly use RaBitQ for each segment, it could lead to a longer quantization time since <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;10</annotation></semantics></math> bits may be used for each dimension of the leading segments and RaBitQ’s indexing time grows exponentially with <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce quantization time, we propose <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">code adjustment quantization</span> (<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CAQ</span>), which reduces the quantization complexity drastically from <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{B}\cdot D\log{D})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, while maintaining the same empirical error and efficiency for distance estimation as RaBitQ. Another special feature of CAQ is, using <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> bits for each dimension, taking first <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> bits (<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;b&lt;B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;b&lt;B</annotation></semantics></math>) for each dimension from the quantized code <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> still forms a valid quantized vector. This provides more opportunities for progressive distance computation, which is not supported in RaBitQ. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> lists the frequently used notations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Frequently used notations in the paper</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">the original data and query vectors</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">the rotated and deducted data and query vectors</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">the quantized vector of <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">the adjusted quantized vector of <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}},\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}},\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">the quantization code of <math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">an integer set with <math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,2,\cdots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,2,\cdots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insights.</span> RaBitQ has high quantization complexity because it requires the quantized vector (and thus vector codewords) <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to have unit norm. This makes the dimensions of <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> dependent as their squared values must sum to 1 and prevents handling each dimension of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
independently.
Such a design is reasonable at first glance since the norm <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is stored explicitly, and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> quantizes the direction vector <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which should have unit norm. However, by inspecting the estimator in Eq (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2. RaBitQ ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we observe that the norm of <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> does not affect estimation. This is, <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> appears in both the numerator and denominator, and thus scaling <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> will not change the estimated value. Instead, <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> should only be required to align with <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in direction. Therefore, we remove the unit norm constraint for <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider each dimension of <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> individually. Intuitively, CAQ works like coordinate descent in optimization, i.e., it starts with a raw quantized vector and then adjusts its dimensions to better align with <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in direction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Quantization Procedure</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Like RaBitQ, CAQ deducts a reference vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> from the data and query vectors and rotates them with a random orthonormal matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the raw data and the query vectors, and the rotated vectors are:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}=P\cdot(\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}),\quad\mathbf{q}=P\cdot(\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}=P\cdot(\mathbf{o}_{r}-\mathbf{c}),\quad\mathbf{q}=P\cdot(\mathbf{q}_{r}-\mathbf{c}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The distance between the original data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and query vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the distance between the rotated data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and query vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is orthonormal. For simplicity, we also call <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math> data and query vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">CAQ initializes the quantized vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> similar with LVQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguerrebere et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. 
In particular, for data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\max}=\max_{i\in[D]}|\mathbf{o}[i]|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\max}=\max_{i\in[D]}|\mathbf{o}[i]|</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the maximum magnitude in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. We divide the range of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-v_{\max},v_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-v_{\max},v_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> uniform intervals, each with length <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=(2\cdot v_{\max})/2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=(2\cdot v_{\max})/2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. The midpoint of the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> interval is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-v_{max}+\Delta(x+0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-v_{max}+\Delta(x+0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is used to quantify the dimensions of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> whose value falls in its corresponding interval. These midpoints form a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional uniform grid, and for each dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[D]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[D]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{c}}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{c}}[i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\left\lfloor\frac{\mathbf{o}[i]+v_{\max}}{\Delta}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\left\lfloor\frac{\mathbf{o}[i]+v_{\max}}{\Delta}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">as the quantization code. Data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantized as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}:=\Delta(\bar{\mathbf{c}}+0.5)-v_{\max}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{D}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}:=\Delta(\bar{\mathbf{c}}+0.5)-v_{\max}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{D}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">LVQ stops here and uses <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate distance.  However, according to RaBitQ’s analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, to approximate the data vector, the quantization vector should align with the data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> in direction. As such, quantization should find the vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with the largest cosine similarity to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})=\frac{\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{o}}{\left\|\mathbf{x}\right\|_{2}\cdot\left\|\mathbf{o}\right\|_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})=\frac{\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{o}}{\left\|\mathbf{x}\right\|_{2}\cdot\left\|\mathbf{o}\right\|_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">LVQ only produce the quantization vector <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>o</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{o}</annotation></semantics></math> that is the nearest to the data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> among all possible quantization vectors and it may not optimize the objection function in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). However, as we have observed, the cosine similarity can be significantly improved by adjusting and refining <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> to better align with the data vector <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. We propose an efficient code adjustment algorithm shown in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Lines 7-11 try to adjust a dimension of the quantized vector by a step of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and see if the direction is better aligned. The adjustment iterates over all dimensions and for a limited number of rounds (Lines 5-6).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates how the code adjustment algorithm adjusts the initial code produced from LVQ to improve cosine similarity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Adjustment of the LVQ Quantized Vector</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span>: round limit <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, data vector <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, start point <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>, value <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output</span>: the final quantized vector <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> for data vector <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            Step size <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\leftarrow(2\cdot v_{\max})/2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\leftarrow(2\cdot v_{\max})/2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            Initialize <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\leftarrow\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\leftarrow\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="round\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">round\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.4.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.6.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.4.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\{\Delta,-\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\{\Delta,-\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.10.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]\in[-v_{\max},v_{\max}]\land\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})&gt;\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}[i]\in[-v_{\max},v_{\max}]\land\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})&gt;\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.13.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></em>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> only changes one vector dimension for each adjustment, we do not need to recompute <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})</annotation></semantics></math> by enumerating all dimensions. Instead, we only need to recompute the contribution of the current dimension to <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime},\mathbf{o})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the time complexity of each adjustment is <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The overall time complexity of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(r\cdot D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(r\cdot D)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is in the same <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(D)</annotation></semantics></math> order for computing <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate the quantization accuracy with different number of adjustment iteration <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in Section  <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Micro Results ‣ 5. EXPERIMENTAL Evaluation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
In practice, we recommend setting <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[4,8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[4,8]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is sufficient to obtain a quantized vector with high quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After adjustment, we obtain the approximate quantization vector <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute the final quantization code <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). The final quantization code is a <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector whose coordinates are <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-bit unsigned integers so that we can store the code with a <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B*D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B*D)</annotation></semantics></math>-bit string. Like RaBitQ, CAQ uses two additonal float numbers for each vector to store the norm and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12086v2/step34-ppted.2.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/243;" width="381" height="243"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The procedure of CAQ for quantization, which first starts with LVQ and then adjusts the quantized vector to align with the data vector in direction.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Distance Estimation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the same distance estimator as RaBitQ, i.e., approximating <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{o},\mathbf{q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{o},\mathbf{q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle/\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle\cdot|\mathbf{o}|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle/\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle\cdot|\mathbf{o}|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. As discussed earlier, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{o}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is precomputed and stored. In the query phase, we can compute <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> using the integer quantization code <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> without decompression as follows</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{D}\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}[i]\cdot\mathbf{q}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐨</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{D}\bar{\mathbf{o}}_{a}[i]\cdot\mathbf{q}[i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{D}\left(\Delta(\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}[i]+0.5)-v_{\max}\right)\cdot\mathbf{q}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{D}\left(\Delta(\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}[i]+0.5)-v_{\max}\right)\cdot\mathbf{q}[i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E13Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\langle\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle+q_{\text{sum}}(-v_{\max}+\Delta/2)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐪</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\langle\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a},\mathbf{q}\rangle+q_{\text{sum}}(-v_{\max}+\Delta/2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{sum}=\sum_{i=1}^{D}\mathbf{q}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{sum}=\sum_{i=1}^{D}\mathbf{q}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, which only needs to be computed once for each query vector. Like RaBitQ, if we quantize each dimension of a query vector to integer, Eq. <a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2. Distance Estimation ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> will mostly use integer computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Progressive distance approximation.</span> CAQ supports progressive distance approximation using the prefix of quantized code of each dimension with an arbitrary length.
In particular, let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the first <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> bits sampled from the native <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> bit quantization code <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in each dimension, that is, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}=\lfloor\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}/2^{B-b}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}=\lfloor\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}/2^{B-b}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. The inner product can be estimated using <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. <a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3.2. Distance Estimation ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> by replacing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}=\Delta\cdot 2^{B-b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}=\Delta\cdot 2^{B-b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our experiments in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Micro Results ‣ 5. EXPERIMENTAL Evaluation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> show that the prefix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{c}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> yields almost the same estimation error as a native CAQ quantized vector using <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>-bits for each dimension. The progressive distance estimator of CAQ enables vector search to conduct progressive distance refinement (e.g., first with 1-bit, then 2-bit, and finally 8-bit). This allows us to provide multiple efficiency-accuracy trade-off options from the same quantization code to satisfy various requirements (e.g., we use it in our multi-stage estimation in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Multi-Stage Distance Estimation ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). In contrast, RaBitQ only supports progressive approximation with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is rather restrictive in its usage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that, to approximate a data vector <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, RaBitQ finds its nearest codeword in codebook <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains unit norm vectors. That is, RaBitQ solves the following optimization problem for quantization</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{G}_{r}}\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{G}_{r}}\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">CAQ finds the codeword that best aligns with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}</annotation></semantics></math> in direction in a codebook <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>CAQ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, CAQ solves the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}}\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>CAQ</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}}\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{o})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">among <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>CAQ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We show in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.3. Analysis ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> that the codebooks of RaBitQ and CAQ are essentially the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 3.1.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">CAQ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of CAQ is equivalent to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in RaBitQ.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Lines 6-11 of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the dimensions of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> take values from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{-v_{\max}+\Delta\cdot(i+0.5)\mid i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{-v_{\max}+\Delta\cdot(i+0.5)\mid i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}=\left\{-v_{\max}+\Delta\cdot(i+0.5)\mid i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}^{D}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>CAQ</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}=\left\{-v_{\max}+\Delta\cdot(i+0.5)\mid i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}^{D}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">With <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=2\cdot v_{\max}/2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=2\cdot v_{\max}/2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, dividing the vectors in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>CAQ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{CAQ}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{-(2^{B}-1)/{2}+i\,\middle|\,i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{-(2^{B}-1)/{2}+i\,\middle|\,i\in[2^{B}-1]\right\}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, which matches the unnormalized codebook <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of RaBitQ in Eq. <a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.3. Extended RaBitQ ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The normalization only changes the norm of the codewords but not the direction, and we have discussed that the norms of the codewords do not affect the estimated inner product.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, if CAQ can solve its optimization problem in Eq (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In 3.3. Analysis ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), it achieves the unbiased estimation and error bound as RaBitQ. Although the coordinate descent style optimization of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.1. Quantization Procedure ‣ 3. Code Adjustment ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> does not necessarily converge to the optimal, empirically, we observe that CAQ achieves identical estimation errors as RaBitQ.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Dimension Segmentation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Segmented CAQ</span> (<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SAQ</span>). SAQ combines the benefits of both <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension balancing</span> and <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension reduction</span> to push the performance limits of vector quantization. In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Motivation and Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we first introduce the motivation of SAQ and formulate the problem of finding a quantization plan. Then we present a dynamic programming algorithm to find the optimal quantization plan in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Quantization Plan Construction ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
For query processing, in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Multi-Stage Distance Estimation ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we show how to use the quantization plan to estimate distance. We also present a multi-stage estimator that facilitates candidate pruning based on the property of the quantization plan.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Motivation and Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CAQ requires the random orthonormal projection because it treats each vector dimension equivalently by quantizing them with the same number of bits. If some dimensions have much larger variances than the others, their accuracy will hurt. In an extreme example, consider a dataset of two-dimensional vectors whose values for the first dimension follow the normal distribution and the values for the second dimension are the same fixed value. The bits assigned for the second dimension will be wasted as we can store one copy of the specific value for all data vectors. By a random projection, the variance is scattered across all dimensions to ensure that all bits are fully utilized to boost accuracy. We refer to this technique as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> dimension balancing</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the reverse direction, if we concentrate the data vectors’ variances into certain dimensions, we can remove the dimensions with negligible variances. PCA is widely used for this purpose. A recent study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> indicates that the high-dimensional vector coordinates, once rotated by a PCA matrix, exhibit a long-tailed variance distribution, which implies that only a few dimensions hold most of the variance (Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1. Motivation and Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). This characteristic was used to create an approximate distance estimator that uses the leading dimensions. These techniques are commonly known as <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dimension reduction</span>.
Dimension reduction is less general, as it is highly dependent on the data distribution. When PCA fails to polarize the variance, a fixed dimension reduction ratio will result in poor accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The common idea behind these two methods indicates that dimensions with higher variance should be allocated more bits and those with lower variance fewer bits. Building on this observation, we introduce <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Segmented CAQ</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SAQ</span>), which uses varying compression ratios based on dimension variance to improve accuracy. Unlike a common parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> for all dimensions as in CAQ, SAQ takes a parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{quota}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{quota}</annotation></semantics></math> to represent the total bit quota to quantize the entire vector. Given a dataset, we first learn a PCA rotation matrix and rotate all data vectors with the PCA matrix. In this way, we polarize the variance and obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance of the values taken by the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions of the rotated vectors. SAQ then performs quantization following a quantization plan, and an example is illustrated in Figure <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1. Motivation and Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel1">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<div id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2509.12086v2/figures/PCA_varance_deep1M.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/172;" width="215" height="172" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">DEEP</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<div id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2509.12086v2/figures/PCA_varance_openai1536.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/173;" width="215" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">OpenAI-1536</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Variance of vector dimension after PCA projection.<span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">figure description</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12086v2/segment-fig-withB.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/168;" width="429" height="168" alt="figure description">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>An illustration of dimension segmentation.<span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">figure description</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To define a quantization plan, we first introduce the concept of a dimension segment <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a set of continuous vector dimensions. For a dimension segment <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x[\mathit{Seg}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x[\mathit{Seg}]</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the projection of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> onto the dimensions in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[\mathit{Seg}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[\mathit{Seg}]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of all such projected vectors.
We further define a tuple <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})</annotation></semantics></math> that indicates that SAQ will quantize each dimension in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{B}</annotation></semantics></math> bits. A quantization plan is given by a set of such tuples <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}:=\{(\mathit{Seg}_{1},\mathit{B}_{1}),(\mathit{Seg}_{2},\mathit{B}_{2}),...\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}:=\{(\mathit{Seg}_{1},\mathit{B}_{1}),(\mathit{Seg}_{2},\mathit{B}_{2}),...\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dimension segment and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{B}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{B}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of bits used to quantize the dimensions in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The union of all these <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Seg}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Seg}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[D]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D]</annotation></semantics></math>. The total bit consumption of this plan is</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P})=\sum_{(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})\in\mathcal{P}}\mathit{B}\cdot|\mathit{Seg}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P})=\sum_{(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})\in\mathcal{P}}\mathit{B}\cdot|\mathit{Seg}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We want to find a quantization plan <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves a low estimation error with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P})\leq Q_{quota}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P})\leq Q_{quota}</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of possible quantization plans can be prohibitively large, and it is difficult to predict the estimation error without deploying and testing the plan with real queries. To this end, we provide an efficient way to model the estimation error of a quantization plan and a method to search for good quantization plan. Note that when quantizing each segment with CAQ, we still apply the random orthonormal projection beforehand such that the dimensions are similar in variance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Quantization Plan Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to our experiments and the analysis of RaBitQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the relative error of quantization a segment (or vector) is proportional to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-B}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of bits used to quantify the segment. This is natural because with 1 more bit, we can reduces the quantization resolution by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we observe that, after PCA projection, the value distribution of a dimension almost follows a normal distribution with a mean close to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#acmlabel3" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2. Quantization Plan Construction ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Therefore, we propose the following expression to model the error introduced in the index phase:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})=\sum_{i\in\mathit{Seg}}\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[|\mathbf{o}_{r}[i]\cdot\mathbf{q}_{r}[i]|\right]}{2^{B+1}}=\frac{1}{2^{B}\pi}\sum_{i\in\mathit{Seg}}\sigma_{i}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ERROR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})=\sum_{i\in\mathit{Seg}}\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[|\mathbf{o}_{r}[i]\cdot\mathbf{q}_{r}[i]|\right]}{2^{B+1}}=\frac{1}{2^{B}\pi}\sum_{i\in\mathit{Seg}}\sigma_{i}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{o}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{o}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are the raw data vector and raw query vector, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> means the value variance of the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th dimension<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Here, we assume query vectors share the same distribution with data vectors. In fact, we can drop the normal distribution assumption and simply use the empirical variance of each dimension (i.e., <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). This will remove the <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In 4.2. Quantization Plan Construction ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). </span></span></span>.
The error introduced by a quantization plan <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be modeled as</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathcal{P})=\sum_{(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})\in\mathcal{P}}\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ERROR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ERROR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑆𝑒𝑔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathcal{P})=\sum_{(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B})\in\mathcal{P}}\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathit{Seg},\mathit{B}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem then becomes finding a plan under the given bit quota <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{quota}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{quota}</annotation></semantics></math> with minimal <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ERROR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ERROR}(\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. To solve this problem, we devise a dynamic programming algorithm as presented in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.2. Quantization Plan Construction ‣ 4. Dimension Segmentation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>Quantization Plan Search</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span>: vector dimension <math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, total bit quota <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{quota}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{quota}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output</span>: Quantization plan <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0,0}\leftarrow(\emptyset,\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0,0}\leftarrow(\emptyset,\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm2.4.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm2.4.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{quota}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{quota}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
