<tr id="S5.T5.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RaBitQ</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">235</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">1958</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">–</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1338</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">2329</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SAQ</span></th>
<td id="S5.T5.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3427</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">3726</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1624</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2812</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.8.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">3112</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.T5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">*<span id="S5.T5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> The maximum recall of SAQ in DEEP is 94.86% when <math id="S5.T5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For SAQ, in the low-dimensional dataset (DEEP), it can achieve a higher recall than RaBitQ at the same compression rate.
In fact, <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>bit SAQ attains a higher recall than <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>bit RaBitQ. However, the QPS of SAQ is hindered by the additional computation of the multi-stage estimator. This is because for low-dimensional data, the computation of an individual estimator is minor and the effect of the early pruning of candidates is negligible, while the constant overhead of the multi-stage estimator is more significant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-stage estimator is more suitable for high-dimensional datasets, where the computation of a single estimator is heavier and the overhead of the multi-stage estimator becomes minor. Early pruning significantly reduces computation and memory access.
Thus, for high-dimension datasets, SAQ consistently outperforms RaBitQ in terms of QPS, recall, and average distance ratio at the same compression rate. Even at <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>bit SAQ can still almost outperform <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>bit RabitQ and produces <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 95\%</annotation></semantics></math> recall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.1. Main Results ‣ 5. EXPERIMENTAL Evaluation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we also report the QPS for different datasets and configurations when 95% recall is achieved. For GIST and OpenAI-1536, SAQ can achieve <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;95\%</annotation></semantics></math> recall and reasonable QPS with <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For MSMARCO, SAQ can also produce a maximum <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> recall with <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficient for SAQ to produce 95% recall for these high-dimensional datasets, and <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=5</annotation></semantics></math> almost gives the best QPS. We notice that for MSMARCO, the QPS of SAQ is <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="14.6\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.6\times</annotation></semantics></math> that of RaBitQ when <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3</annotation></semantics></math>. This significant difference occurs because RaBitQ approaches its maximum recall at <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=3</annotation></semantics></math> due to error limitations, causing a sharp decline in its QPS, while SAQ maintains high performance with room for further recall improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Micro Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Space consumption.</span>
We first report the space consumption of SAQ and RaBitQ with different configurations <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The space includes the quantization code, two extra factors per data vector and other factors that consume constant space. We only report the results from the MSMARCO dataset under a few configurations of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The space consumption is proportional to the number of data vectors and the number of dimensions for the other datasets, and also proportional to other configurations of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As reported in Table<a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.2. Micro Results ‣ 5. EXPERIMENTAL Evaluation ‣ SAQ: Pushing the Limits of Vector Quantization through Code Adjustment and Dimension Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the space consumption is almost proportional to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that, due to the constant overhead of some factors, the compression rate may not be as expected in small <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The space consumption of SAQ is slightly larger than the baseline since the multi-stage estimator of SAQ requires to store more statistics of the dataset and consume more space for extra factors. However, this overhead is negligible for large-scale datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6. </span>Storage space of the quantized vectors for MSMARCO. ‘-’: the configuration not supported by the method.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
